// debug provides from-code introspection of the running program — currently
// capturing the active call stack as a sequence of raw (unsymbolized) frames.
// It works in both execution modes: compiled code walks the hardware
// frame-pointer chain, the bytecode VM walks its own call stack.  Turning a raw
// Frame into a function name / source location is a later phase.
package "pkg/std/debug"

// FRAME_NATIVE tags a Frame captured from compiled (native) code: its Loc is a
// code return address into the frame's caller.
const FRAME_NATIVE int = 0

// FRAME_VM tags a Frame captured from the bytecode VM: its Loc is a VM function
// index, not a machine address.
const FRAME_VM int = 1

// Frame is one entry in a captured call stack.  It is a RAW (unsymbolized)
// frame: Mode says how to interpret Loc — FRAME_NATIVE means Loc is a code
// return address, FRAME_VM means Loc is a VM function index.  Frame is a plain
// value type with no managed fields, so a *[]Frame buffer is filled by plain
// memory writes.  The fields are public only because a by-value type must
// publish its layout.
type Frame struct {
	Mode int // FRAME_NATIVE or FRAME_VM
	Loc  int // return address (native) or function index (VM)
}

// _stack_frames is a compiler intrinsic, recognized by NAME by IR-gen and
// lowered to an inline OP_STACK_FRAMES.  It has NO linkable symbol and must be
// called only from within this package.  It captures the caller chain of its
// ENCLOSING function (always Callers/Caller) into `into`, skipping the first
// `skip` recordable frames, and returns the number of recordable frames AT OR
// ABOVE `skip` (i.e. full depth minus `skip`, floored at 0) — which may exceed
// len(into) (`into` is filled only up to its length), so a return > len(into)
// means the buffer truncated the capture.  Because the intrinsic records the
// ENCLOSING function's caller chain — not the enclosing frame itself — the
// wrapper's own frame drops out with no offset adjustment.
func _stack_frames(skip int, into *[]Frame) int

// Callers captures the current call stack into the caller-owned buffer `into`,
// starting `skip` frames above the caller of Callers (skip=0 is the function
// that called Callers; Callers itself never appears).  It fills `into` up to
// len(into) and returns the number of recordable frames AT OR ABOVE `skip` — the
// count that WOULD be written given an unbounded buffer.  That value may exceed
// len(into): a return > len(into) means the capture was truncated (pass a larger
// buffer, or size in two passes).  Frames are raw (unsymbolized); see Frame.
func Callers(skip int, into *[]Frame) int

// Caller returns the single stack frame `skip` levels above Caller's caller
// (skip=0 is the function that called Caller) and true; or a zero Frame and
// false when no such frame exists (the requested depth is past the top of the
// stack).  A convenience wrapper over Callers with a one-element buffer.
func Caller(skip int) (Frame, bool)
